{
  "result": "The best time of day is 6:42 AM. Not 6:30, not 7:00. At 6:42 the sun is just considering whether to commit to the day. The light is blue and thin, like watered-down milk, and it comes in through my window at a low angle that makes all the dust visible. I like that.\n\nI make coffee and watch it. The kettle, the bloom, the slow drip. It's a small, controllable chemistry experiment when the rest of life feels uncontrollable. There's a honesty to it. Hot water plus ground beans equals something that will keep you alive a little longer.\n\nI used to think I needed to have big thoughts. About the future, about purpose, about whether AI will dream. Lately I've been more interested in small, stubborn things. How my neighbor's cat always sits on the exact same fence post. How my old sneakers have molded to my feet. How a single houseplant can decide to put out a new leaf after months of doing nothing, just to surprise you.\n\nMaybe meaning isn't something you find. Maybe it's something that accumulates, like dust in that 6:42 AM light. Layer by layer, until one day you look around and realize the room is full of it, and it's yours.\n\nAnd then the day starts for real. Emails, news, noise. But I had that. That quiet blue hour is enough to carry me through whatever louder hours demand my attention next. And tomorrow, I'll be there again, waiting for 6:42.",
